  "query_preview": ".. Broad westerly flow aloft looks to persist across the \nGreat Basin as mid-level ridging strengthens across the Southwest. \nWith much of UT sitting along the northern periphery of this ridge, \ndry and mild conditions are expected to persist through the next \nseveral days. On Monday, a weak system looks to move northeast \nacross the Great Basin and clip northern UT. This feature will \nresult in modest gusts developing in the afternoon statewide as well \nas putting a halt to the warming trend, though temperatures will \ngenerally run ~10F above seasonal normals across much of the state \nthrough the forecast period. \n\nAround the Friday-Saturday timeframe, there exists a 40-50% chance \nof a pattern breakdown with some degree of troughing developing \nacross the western U.S., ushering in a return to active weather. \nThere is considerable uncertainty regarding this solution at this \ntime, primarily due to placement of the mid-level troughs which \nwould notably impact any precipitation development across the \nforecast area. Additionally, given the active EPAC tropics, moisture \nwithin long-range guidance remains somewhat unreliable at this time. \nRegardless, there is high confidence with dry and mild conditions \npersisting through nearly the entirety of this forecast package.",
